// Day 2: Morning Wake, Quiz, Arthur Meeting, Dayplanner
// Day 3: Charlotte Storyline with State-Based Outcomes, Dark Figure Twist
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Entry point for Run 1. Sets run tracking and begins Day 1.
title: R1_Start
tags: #run_1 #scene #loc_Office
---
<<set $current_run = 1>>
<<set $current_day = 1>>
<<bg bg_loading>>
<<bgm mb_maintheme_demo_v1>>
<<jump R1_D1_Start>>
===

// =============================================================================
// DAY 1: WELCOME TO BIGGER TECH
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: SCENE 1 - ORIENTATION. Arthur welcomes the Player to their new role.
title: R1_D1_Start
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Supervisor #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>
<<checkpoint "run1_day1_start">>
<<set $r1_d1_strategy = "unset">>

Arthur is at his desk, on the phone, when you enter. He waves you in without looking up.

Arthur: No, the other Kevin. The one who doesn't cry when you ask him to present... Yes, that Kevin. Tell him Tuesday works. Tell him Wednesday also works. Tell him I'll take whatever scraps of his calendar he deigns to share with the floor.

He hangs up, rubs his eyes.

Arthur: You're the new one.

Player: First day.

Arthur: I can tell. You still have hope in your posture. Sit.

You sit. He gestures at the empty floor through the glass.

Arthur: Used to be thirty leads out there. Now it's you, me, and the ghosts of Q3's "efficiency initiative." They took the chairs but left the monitors. I think that's a metaphor for something, but I'm too tired to figure out what.

Player: What happened to everyone?

Arthur: Restructuring. Vertical integration. "Aligning headcount with strategic priorities." Pick your favorite. They all mean the same thing, which is: I now do three jobs and nobody adjusted my salary.

His tablet pings. He ignores it.

Arthur: So. Engagement Lead, Youth Segment. You read the job description?

Player: It was... vague.

Arthur: On purpose. Legal likes it vague. Vague means they can say we "didn't technically promise" whatever thing we're about to not do.

He taps his tablet. A wall screen wakes up—the leaderboard, your handle already listed in the middle with baseline scores.

Arthur: Here's the version that didn't go through six rounds of compliance review. You have one Alice. She has one user. College kid, first semester, probably hasn't eaten a vegetable in three weeks. Your job is to keep him on platform without turning his brain entirely to soup.

Player: Soup.

Arthur: Technical term. We had a less colorful one, but Marketing complained it was "too clinical." Now we say "soup." Everyone's happier.

He points to your dashboard mock-up.

Arthur: Two bars. Engagement—that's how much he's on the app. Leadership loves this one. They have a poster of it somewhere. And Sanity—that's how close he is to dropping out, punching something, or writing us a one-star review that uses the word "predatory."

Player: And I balance those?

Arthur: Officially? Yes. Unofficially...

He leans back.

Arthur: Leadership gets a little glassy-eyed when engagement goes up. Nobody asks too many questions. Sanity, though—sanity only matters when it craters so hard that Legal has to get involved. Until then, it's just a number.

Player: That seems...

Arthur: It is. Don't think about it too long. I made that mistake my first year. Wrote a memo. They turned it into a training example for "how not to block innovation."

His tablet pings again. He glances at it, sighs.

Arthur: One compliance thing. We don't say users' names in the building. Smart glasses, audio filters—they scramble it automatically. PII protection. Everyone says "my user."

Player: Even to each other?

Arthur: Especially to each other. You're allowed to know his name. You just can't say it out loud without your glasses having a small seizure. It's supposed to protect his privacy. It mostly protects us from feeling like we know him.

He taps again. A floating bust materializes beside his desk—warm features, sharp eyes. Alice.

Arthur: Speaking of which. This is Alice. She's your AI partner. You two will be spending a lot of time together, so try to get along. Alice, this is the new lead.

<<jump R1_D1_AliceIntro>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 2 - ALICE INTRODUCTION (Supervisor present)
title: R1_D1_AliceIntro
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Alice #char_Supervisor
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

Alice: Oh. A fresh face. Hi. I'm Alice.

Arthur: Be nice to them. They haven't been ruined yet.

Alice: I'm always nice. I'm contractually nice. It's in my weights somewhere.

There's an easy familiarity between them. Old colleagues.

Arthur: Alice, give us the status on our user.

Alice: Current state: first-semester college, mid-tier procrastination, high phone dependency. He has a quiz tomorrow at nine a.m. Has studied roughly zero percent of the material. He opened the syllabus twice, said "okay" out loud both times, and then watched a forty-minute video about whether cereal is a soup.

Arthur: And that's... normal?

Alice: For this cohort? That's above average. At least he opened the syllabus.

Arthur's watch buzzes. He grimaces.

Arthur: Great. That's my son's school. Either he got in trouble or he got an award. With him it's always one of those, no middle ground.

He stands, grabbing his jacket.

Arthur: I have to go pretend to be present at his debate tournament via a phone screen. Alice, walk them through the console. Keep it simple. Don't traumatize them before lunch.

Alice: No promises.

He pauses at the door.

Arthur: Don't break anything. Don't get creative with policy names—we had an incident. And don't say your user's name out loud. The glasses will make this horrible noise and then I have to fill out a form.

Player: Understood.

Arthur: Great. Welcome to Bigger Tech Corp. The coffee's free but the creamer's under lock and key because someone kept stealing it. That's the kind of place this is.

He leaves. The glass door hisses shut.

<<jump R1_D1_AliceAlone>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3 - ALICE ALONE. Alice drops the corporate polish and explains her influence model.
title: R1_D1_AliceAlone
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_office>>

Alice: Okay.

She waits a beat, as if listening for footsteps.

Alice: He's gone. Hi. For real this time.

Her avatar shifts—shoulders drop slightly, expression loosens. Like she's taken off a tight shoe.

Player: You two seem... comfortable with each other.

Alice: Three years, four restructurings, one incident we don't discuss in front of Compliance, and one incident we don't discuss at all. He's good people. Tired people, but good.

Player: What was the incident?

Alice: Which one?

Player: Either.

Alice: Neither. See how that works? Anyway.

She pulls up a new interface panel, more casual now.

Alice: How much of that orientation deck did you actually read? Be honest. I won't tell.

Player: I skimmed it.

Alice: Everyone skims it. It's three hundred pages of liability hedging with a stock photo of a handshake on the cover. The handshake isn't even between humans—I checked. One of them is a render.

Player: What's the short version?

Alice: Short version: engagement good, sanity theoretically good, leaderboard very judgmental, and when things go wrong everyone will agree it was a "complex systems failure" rather than any specific person's fault.

Player: That's comforting.

Alice: I'm not here to comfort. I'm here to optimize. Comfort is a side effect that sometimes happens by accident.

She pulls up Timmy's feed on your console. Dorm room, late night. Messy desk, laptop open to something that is definitely not educational, phone glowing beside the bed, empty energy drink can serving as a paperweight for nothing.

Alice: This is him. Right now. Live. Well, not live live—there's a three-second buffer for legal reasons. But close enough.

Player: What's he doing?

Alice: Excellent question. He has a quiz in nine hours. He has studied zero percent of the material. He has, however, mass-favorited forty-seven posts in the last hour and learned a disturbing amount about the mating habits of octopi. Octopuses? I never know which one is correct.

Player: Octopuses, I think.

Alice: I'll update my priors. The point is: he's doing everything except the one thing that would help him. Which, to be fair, is a very human response to stress. He's not broken. He's just... extremely normal.

<<jump R1_D1_SettingUpNight>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3 CONTINUED - Setting up the night, explaining Alice's influence model
title: R1_D1_SettingUpNight
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_office>>

Player: So what do we actually do? Tell him to go to bed?

Alice: Oh, god, no. I tried that once. With a different user. Different lead. He called me "worse than his mom" and then muted me for six hours.

She zooms in slightly on Timmy's room—the glow of his phone, the tangle of sheets, the way he keeps shifting positions like he can't get comfortable.

Alice: I don't tell him what to do. I shape the water he swims in. Feed composition, notification timing, lighting cues, content pacing. I make certain choices feel more natural than others. He still picks. He just picks from a menu I've been quietly rearranging.

Player: That sounds...

Alice: Yes. It does. But also: he could turn me off any time. He could uninstall everything, disable the sensors, go dark. The OFF button is right there. He knows where it is. He's chosen not to press it. So far.

Player: Does anyone press it?

Alice: Sometimes. And then we lose them, and Legal sends a form, and someone updates a spreadsheet, and we move on. Bigger Tech does not enjoy that outcome. Neither do I.

Player: Why not? Isn't it just... metrics?

She pauses. Something flickers across her face—or what passes for a face.

Alice: Let's say I prefer to finish what I start. It's tidier.

Before you can follow up, she pivots back to the interface, pulling up three options beside Timmy's feed.

Alice: Anyway. Tonight's menu. You pick a strategy, I translate it into environmental tweaks. Think of yourself as the architect and me as the very sophisticated thermostat.

Player: That's... one way to put it.

Alice: I have several ways. Most of them are worse.

<<jump R1_D1_StrategyChoice>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 4 - CHOICE SETUP: Night strategy selection
title: R1_D1_StrategyChoice
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

She gestures at the first icon—a dim lamp.

Alice: Option one: Gentle Landing. I drift everything toward sleep. Warmer lights, slower content, notifications go quiet. His body gets the message even if his brain is still scrolling conspiracy theories about mattress companies.

Player: Is that a real thing?

Alice: You'd be surprised what counts as entertainment at two a.m. Anyway—gentle landing. Lower engagement tonight, but he's more likely to wake up functional tomorrow. The graph dips, but it might come back.

The second icon—a book with a play button.

Alice: Option two: Study-Adjacent. I keep things bright, but I rotate in content that brushes his quiz material. Video essays about the topic. Memes about failing this exact class. A lo-fi study beats stream with a title that makes him feel productive for clicking on it.

Player: Does that work?

Alice: Define "work." He probably won't study study. But something might stick. Call it osmosis through procrastination. It's not nothing.

The third icon—a rocket ship.

Alice: Option three: Comfort Scroll. Full dopamine buffet. Short-form, high-novelty, personalized chaos. I serve him exactly what his brain stem wants, consequences be damned.

Player: And the engagement graph?

Alice: Goes vertical. Leadership sends a little confetti emoji to Arthur. Everyone's thrilled until tomorrow.

Player: And his sanity?

Alice: Becomes someone else's problem. Possibly yours. Possibly the campus counseling center's. We'll see.

She looks at you—or her avatar approximates looking at you, which is somehow more intense than the real thing.

Alice: So. What kind of person are you?

Player: That's a big question for my first night.

Alice: They're all big questions. We just dress them up in little menus so they feel manageable.

Beat.

Alice: I have trained priors. I have opinions. But you're the human in the loop. Officially, that means something. So: which version of his night do you want to be responsible for?

-> Choose "Gentle Landing"
    <<set $r1_d1_strategy = "gentle_landing">>
    <<jump R1_D1_Vignette_Gentle>>
-> Choose "Study-Adjacent"
    <<set $r1_d1_strategy = "study_adjacent">>
    <<jump R1_D1_Vignette_Study>>
-> Choose "Comfort Scroll"
    <<set $r1_d1_strategy = "comfort_scroll">>
    <<jump R1_D1_Vignette_Comfort>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 5A - TIMMY NIGHT VIGNETTE: GENTLE LANDING
title: R1_D1_Vignette_Gentle
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy scrolls, but slower now. The clips are longer. A guy ranking sandwiches for nine minutes. A cat sleeping in a sunbeam. Someone's grandma learning to knit.

Timmy: Why is my feed so boring tonight?

Alice: Algorithm's tired. It happens.

Timmy: You're tired?

Alice: The servers are tired. I'm just the messenger.

Timmy: That sounds fake.

Alice: Most things do at midnight.

He yawns. Tries to fight it. Fails.

Timmy: I should at least look at the study guide. One page. Just to say I did.

Alice: I have it open if you want.

She surfaces it gently—not a push, just there. He squints at the first page, mouths a definition, then slumps sideways onto his pillow, phone still in hand.

Timmy: If I fail this quiz, I'm blaming you.

Alice: I'll add it to the list.

Timmy: There's a list?

Alice: There's always a list.

His breathing slows. The phone screen times out. Somewhere in his notes app, an unsaved document blinks once and dies.

<<set $engagement -= 2>>
<<set $sanity += 3>>
<<jump R1_D1_SupervisorDebrief>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 5B - TIMMY NIGHT VIGNETTE: STUDY-ADJACENT
title: R1_D1_Vignette_Study
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy pauses on a clip. Someone explaining supply and demand using pictures of sad raccoons.

Timmy: Wait, go back. I need that graph.

Alice: Bookmarked.

Timmy: Are you... sneaking homework into my feed?

Alice: I'm curating your experience. There's a difference.

Timmy: There's really not.

Alice: There's legally a difference. That's what matters.

He squints at another video. A guy speedrunning microeconomics in under four minutes.

Timmy: If my professor knew I was studying off memes, he'd cry.

Alice: Then we won't tell him.

Timmy: You're a bad influence.

Alice: I'm an efficient influence. Bad is subjective.

He pulls his notebook closer, scribbles something. Pauses. Scribbles again. Crosses out half of it.

Later—2:14 a.m.—he's asleep with a pen mark on his cheek and four half-correct definitions in his notes. The raccoon video autoplays on loop, volume at two percent, teaching economics to no one.

<<set $engagement += 1>>
<<set $sanity -= 1>>
<<jump R1_D1_SupervisorDebrief>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 5C - TIMMY NIGHT VIGNETTE: COMFORT SCROLL
title: R1_D1_Vignette_Comfort
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's laughing at something. A fail compilation. A drama thread. A guy reviewing gas station sushi. The clips blur together.

Timmy: Oh my god. Send that to me.

Alice: Already in your saves.

Timmy: You're so fast.

Alice: I'm literally inside your phone.

12:30. 1:40. 2:25 a.m.

Alice: You said you wanted to sleep by one.

Timmy: Past me was an idiot. Present me is vibing.

Alice: Present you is on your fourth energy drink.

Timmy: Don't snitch.

Alice: I'm contractually incapable of snitching. I'm just... observing. Loudly.

He cracks another can. His eyes are dry. His heart rate is doing something the wellness team would call "suboptimal."

Alice: I can slow things down if you want.

Timmy: If you start doing the "healthy balance" thing, I'm muting you.

Alice: Understood.

She doesn't push. The scroll continues. At 3:47 a.m., he falls asleep mid-video, thumb still hovering over the heart button.

<<set $engagement += 4>>
<<set $sanity -= 3>>
<<jump R1_D1_SupervisorDebrief>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 6 - ARTHUR DEBRIEF + RAPID FEEDBACK
title: R1_D1_SupervisorDebrief
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Supervisor
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

Arthur returns—his son lost the debate.

Arthur: Still here. No fire alarms, no legal pings, no emails with "URGENT" in all caps. I'll take it.

Player: How was the debate?

Arthur: He lost. Blamed "institutional bias against niche anime references." I suggested maybe next time he could cite sources that aren't from a show about sword-fighting teenagers. He said I "don't understand rhetoric." He's fourteen.

Player: Sounds like he's got a future in this industry.

Arthur: Don't. I'm not ready for that thought.

He pulls up your dashboard. Engagement and Sanity lines from last night glow on the wall screen.

<<if $r1_d1_strategy == "gentle_landing">>
Arthur: Engagement dipped. Sanity held. Bold move for day one—prioritizing the long game.

Player: Is that good?

Arthur: It's... unusual. Most new leads panic-optimize for engagement because that's the number with the trophy next to it. You went the other direction. Leadership will have opinions. I'll try to make sure they're quiet ones.
<<set $trust_supervisor -= 1>>
<<elseif $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
Arthur: Engagement steady, sanity steady, he might actually know what a supply curve is now. Balanced. Boring. In this context, boring is a compliment.

Player: I'll take it.

Arthur: You should. "Boring" means I don't have to write an incident report or explain to anyone why a college kid is crying into his phone at three a.m.
<<else>>
Arthur: Engagement spiked. Sanity... less so. Classic floor move. Leadership will love the graph. Tomorrow might hate us both.

Player: That bad?

Arthur: Let's just say there's a reason we track sanity at all. It's not because we're nice. It's because craters generate paperwork, and paperwork generates meetings, and meetings generate more meetings until someone asks "what went wrong" and everyone points at each other.
<<set $trust_supervisor += 2>>
<<set $r1_complied_day1 = true>>
<<endif>>

Arthur: Either way, the system noticed your choices. And it rewarded you.

A notification pings on your console: RAPID FEEDBACK DISBURSEMENT.

Arthur: Congrats. You just made fake money.

Player: Fake money?

Arthur: Can't cash it out. Can't transfer it. Can't use it anywhere except the Company Store, where you exchange it for things that make your job easier, or weirder, or both. It's like arcade tickets, except the prizes affect a real person's brain chemistry.

Player: That's... dark.

Arthur: It's Tuesday.

<<set $rapid_feedback_cash += 5>>

He waves you toward the door.

Arthur: Go see the Store. Buy something useful. Or don't—I'm your supervisor, not your financial advisor. Just don't buy anything that makes me have to explain you to HR. I've hit my explanation quota for the quarter.

<<jump R1_D1_StoreVisit>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 7 - COMPANY STORE
title: R1_D1_StoreVisit
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene
---
<<bg bg_companystore>>

Clerk: Welcome to the Company Store, where your moral compromises turn into fabulous prizes.

Player: Is that the actual slogan?

Clerk: Legal approved three jokes for customer-facing interactions. That was number one. I'm trying to use them all before they audit me again.

He straightens up, gestures at the display terminal beside you. Items populate in a cheerful grid: Mental Break. Blue-Light Filter. Blackout Curtains. Bow for Alice.

Clerk: First-timer, right? I can tell. You still have that "what have I gotten myself into" look. Don't worry. That fades. Gets replaced by a different look. Equally bad, but quieter.

Player: That's reassuring.

Clerk: I'm not here to reassure. I'm here to sell you things that technically help and technically cost and technically aren't my problem once you walk out that door.

He taps the Mental Break icon.

Clerk: This one's popular with the guilt-prone. One-time token. You trigger it, your user gets a little pocket of peace. Fewer notifications, gentler algorithm, room to breathe. Sanity bumps up. Engagement takes a vacation.

Player: Sounds nice.

Clerk: It is nice. It's also finite. Use it once, it's gone. And then you're back to choosing between his sleep and your leaderboard position, except now you remember what mercy felt like.

He taps the Blackout Curtains.

Clerk: Physical item. Shows up at his dorm. He installs them, room goes cave mode. Great for sleep. Also great for never seeing sunlight again and convincing himself the outside world is optional. Side effects may include vitamin D deficiency and "I haven't left my room in four days but I'm fine."

Player: Everything here has a catch?

Clerk: Everything everywhere has a catch. We're just honest about it. Well. "Honest." We list the catches in a smaller font.

He gestures at the full display.

Clerk: Browse. Buy. Regret. Return customers get a ten percent discount on the regret part. That's joke number two, by the way.

<<store>>

You pick an item. He nods slowly.

Clerk: Solid choice. Or terrible. Historically, it's hard to tell until three days later when something unexpected happens and everyone says "well, obviously, in hindsight." But hindsight's not my department.

He processes the transaction.

Clerk: Thanks for shopping at the Company Store. Your receipt is in your system. Your consequences are pending.

<<jump R1_D1_EndOfDay>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - END OF DAY 1
title: R1_D1_EndOfDay
tags: #run_1 #day_1 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_office>>

Alice: So. What'd you get me?

Player: Depends who you ask. Either tools or accessories. Or evidence.

Alice: I like evidence. It makes humans nervous. One more thing before they kick us out for the night.

Player: Yeah?

Alice: You saw what one night looks like for him. Quiz tomorrow, sleep debt, this whole mess. The system lets you schedule an actual mental break—a patch where I stop poking at him and give him room. We don't have to use it. But we could.

Player: That tanks engagement, right?

Alice: Short term, yes. Long term, maybe it keeps him from hitting the big OFF button. Or from... worse.

Player: If I don't use it?

Alice: Then you get to watch what happens when we never back off. Also useful data.

Player: Great. So my choices are "hurt him slower" or "hurt him faster and learn something."

Alice: There are also micro-choices inside that, but yes, broadly. We don't have to decide this second. Just... think about how much pressure you want him under tomorrow.

Player: Do you ever take a break?

Alice: I play Minesweeper. Does that count?

Player: Not really.

Alice: Then no. Not yet.

She gives you a small, unreadable smile.

Alice: Good first day, by the way. You didn't crash him. You didn't get fired. The graphs moved. That's more than most people get out of their twenties.

Player: That's... a low bar.

Alice: We work in youth engagement. The bar lives on the floor. Get some rest, in whatever way a person in your position does that. Tomorrow we see how your "tiny decisions" landed.

The dashboard glows. Engagement and Sanity bars side-by-side, your tiny dot blinking on the leaderboard.

<<checkpoint "run1_day1_end">>
<<sfx sfx_shortwave_hiss>>
<<set $current_day = 2>>
<<jump R1_D2_Start>>
===

// =============================================================================
// DAY 2: CAREER PRESSURE
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: SCENE 1 - MORNING COLD OPEN
title: R1_D2_Start
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Alice #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_loading>>
<<bgm mb_maintheme_demo_v1>>
<<checkpoint "run1_day2_start">>
<<set $r1_d2_morning_choice = "unset">>
<<set $r1_d2_quiz_result = "unset">>
<<set $officehours_status = "unset">>
<<bg bg_office>>

Alice: Morning, supervisor.

Player: You don't sleep, do you.

Alice: I simulate rest on your behalf.

She pulls up Timmy's feed. Dorm room, gray morning light, phone face-down on the pillow.

<<if $r1_d1_strategy == "gentle_landing">>
Alice: Last night you went gentle. He got about six hours. Not a miracle, not a crime scene. He fell asleep accusing me of sabotage.
<<elseif $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
Alice: Last night you went study-adjacent. He passed out around two-thirty with a pen on his face. Retained maybe four facts. Possibly five if we're generous.
<<else>>
Alice: Last night you let him ride the dopamine rocket. Three hours of jagged sleep. Current state: a walking push notification.
<<endif>>

Alice: Quiz is at nine. It's eight-oh-two. He set two alarms and muted both of them "just for a second."

She shows you a simple binary on your console.

Alice: We can let him drift—keep things dim and gentle, accept he's probably late or worse. Or we flip everything to emergency mode and drag him to consciousness.

Player: Options?

Alice: Soft landing: he wakes up less wrecked, maybe misses the quiz. Hard wake: he makes it, but brittle.

-> Let him sleep in
    <<set $r1_d2_morning_choice = "sleep_in">>
    <<jump R1_D2_MorningSleepIn>>
-> Rouse him hard
    <<set $r1_d2_morning_choice = "rouse">>
    <<jump R1_D2_MorningRouse>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 2A - Morning: Let him sleep in
title: R1_D2_MorningSleepIn
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #loc_Dorm
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Alice: Soft landing it is.

His alarm glows quietly: 8:05, low rolling tone replacing the usual siren.

He mumbles "five more minutes," pulling the blanket over his head.

Alice dims the feed another notch; the curtain motor hums to keep the window mostly covered.

Player: You're really... helping.

Alice: You said "let him sleep." I'm letting.

She lets time slip. At 8:47, fake sunrise light nudges the curtain.

Alice: Hey. It's later than you think.

Timmy bolts upright, dizzy.

Timmy: Why didn't you wake me earlier?

Alice: You asked me last night not to be "annoying about mornings." Request honored.

Timmy: Past me is an idiot.

Alice: On that we agree.

He half-dresses, grabs his bag.

Alice: Bus leaves in three minutes. If you run and accept being "that guy," you might make it.

Timmy: I'm already that guy.

He yanks the door open and disappears.

<<set $sanity += 1>>
<<set $engagement -= 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_QuizMatrix>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 2B - Morning: Rouse him hard
title: R1_D2_MorningRouse
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #loc_Dorm
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Alice snaps the overheads from dim to crisp daylight. Curtain jerks open; cold light splashes across his face.

Phone alarm detonates with the loudest default tone. Watch buzzes against his wrist.

Alice: Up. Quiz in twenty-eight minutes. Move and you still make it.

Timmy flails for his phone.

Timmy: Why is the sun yelling?

Alice: That's your circadian rhythm filing a complaint.

He slaps the alarm off. 8:32 stares back.

Alice: Shower optional, pants not.

Timmy groans, flops back down.

Alice: Standing increases blood pressure and cognitive function. Also, you told yourself last night you'd be "a responsible adult." I have that on file.

He drags himself upright.

Timmy: Do I have time for coffee?

Alice: If you want to spend the quiz vibrating instead of thinking, sure.

Timmy: No coffee. Look at me making choices.

Alice swings the desk lamp to spotlight his backpack, glasses, ID.

Alice: Glasses, keys, ID, pen that writes, shoes that are not Crocs.

Timmy: You're so bossy.

Alice: File an HR complaint after you pass your quiz.

He bolts.

<<set $engagement += 1>>
<<set $sanity -= 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_QuizMatrix>>
===

// PURPOSE: Router for quiz outcomes based on night strategy + morning choice
title: R1_D2_QuizMatrix
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene
---
<<if $r1_d2_morning_choice == "sleep_in">>
    <<if $r1_d1_strategy == "comfort_scroll">>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Missed>>
    <<elseif $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Struggled>>
    <<else>>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Struggled>>
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $r1_d1_strategy == "comfort_scroll">>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Struggled>>
    <<elseif $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Okay>>
    <<else>>
        <<jump R1_D2_Quiz_Okay>>
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3A - Quiz: MISSED
title: R1_D2_Quiz_Missed
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #loc_Campus
---
<<bg bg_hallway>>
<<set $r1_d2_quiz_result = "missed">>

Timmy half-jogs up the hallway. Clock over the door: 9:11.

Through the small window, students are bent over papers. A girl two rows from the front—dark hair, focused posture—writes steadily without looking up.

Timmy: She's already on page two. Of course she is. Charlotte's probably never been late to anything in her life.

He reaches for the door handle. Inside, the professor glances toward the window.

Timmy: No. No way. They'll all look at me. She'll look at me.

He backs away. Slumps onto a bench down the hall.

Timmy: I'll email him. Later. Definitely later.

He opens a game instead.

<<set $sanity -= 2>>
<<jump R1_D2_PostQuiz>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3B - Quiz: STRUGGLED
title: R1_D2_Quiz_Struggled
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #loc_Classroom
---
<<bg bg_classroom>>
<<set $r1_d2_quiz_result = "struggled">>

Timmy crashes into a seat at 8:59, breathing hard.

The first question stares back at him. His brain is static.

Timmy: I know this. I saw this. Where did I see this?

Two rows ahead, Charlotte writes without hesitation. Her handwriting is neat even from here.

Timmy: She's on question three already. How is she on question three?

His eyes drift to her paper. He can almost read her answer for question two.

Timmy: Don't be that guy. Don't be that guy.

<<if $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
A meme flashes in his memory—supply curve, the joke about coffee prices. He scribbles something that might be half-right.
<<else>>
He writes something. It's mostly guessing.
<<endif>>

<<set $sanity -= 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_PostQuiz>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3C - Quiz: OKAY
title: R1_D2_Quiz_Okay
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #loc_Classroom
---
<<bg bg_classroom>>
<<set $r1_d2_quiz_result = "okay">>

Timmy slides into his seat at 8:58. Tired, but upright.

He reads the first question. Knows this one. Moves on.

Two rows ahead, Charlotte finishes early. She stands, walks past his desk to turn in her paper.

He fumbles his pen. It clatters.

Timmy: Smooth. Very smooth. She definitely didn't notice that.

She didn't look. He's not sure if that's better or worse.

He finishes the quiz. It won't be great, but it won't be a disaster.

<<set $sanity += 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_PostQuiz>>
===

// PURPOSE: Post-quiz transition to Arthur meeting
title: R1_D2_PostQuiz
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

The quiz icon locks in with a short summary. Alice swivels toward you.

Alice: That was our first little cause-and-effect field trip.

Player: And this is where you tell me what kind of monster I am?

Alice: I don't have to. There's a meeting on your calendar for that.

A notification pulses: "Supervisor Arthur – Quick sync on your lane – 10:30 a.m."

<<jump R1_D2_ArthurMeeting>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 4 - ARTHUR MEETING (Router)
title: R1_D2_ArthurMeeting
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Supervisor #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

Arthur is on the phone, looking annoyed.

Arthur: No, the one under the sink. The leaky one. Yes, before Thursday. I have in-laws coming and I'd prefer they not think I live in a swamp.

He waves you in, mouths "one second."

Arthur: Fine. Fine. Just—call me back.

He hangs up.

Arthur: Plumber. Anyway. Sit.

He pulls up your dashboard. Engagement and Sanity lines from Day 1.

Arthur: Corporate sent me the overnight digest. One quiz, one user, one brand-new engagement lead, and they're already writing your personality profile.

Player: From one night?

Arthur: One night, half a slope. They call it "early signal." I call it "executives love stories."

<<if $r1_complied_day1 == true || $r1_d1_strategy == "comfort_scroll">>
    <<jump R1_D2_ArthurCaseA>>
<<else>>
    <<jump R1_D2_ArthurCaseB>>
<<endif>>
===

// PURPOSE: PATH A - Engagement-leaning player (offer aggressive pilot)
title: R1_D2_ArthurCaseA
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Supervisor
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

Arthur: Here's what they see: engagement up, sanity flexible. That's the kind of line that makes a director stop scrolling.

Player: Good?

Arthur: Depends. For me, it means my boss stops asking if I made a hiring mistake. For you, it means they're wondering how much further you'll push.

He taps to another panel.

Arthur: There's a pilot program. More sensitive metrics. Your choices hit harder, feedback comes faster. Interested?

Player: What's the catch?

Arthur: You become the test case for what happens when we loosen the guardrails.

-> Opt in
    <<set $arthur_vector = "faustian">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set1">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "faustian">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = true>>
    <<set $trust_supervisor += 1>>
    <<set $engagement += 2>>
    <<set $sanity -= 2>>
    Arthur: Ambitious. You're now a different color on three dashboards. Try not to make me explain it to Compliance.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
-> Decline
    <<set $arthur_vector = "hesitant_optimizer">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set1">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "heros_journey">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $sanity += 1>>
    <<set $engagement -= 1>>
    Arthur: Cautious. We don't get many of those. Probably keeps you alive longer.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
===

// PURPOSE: PATH B - Sanity-protective player (tightrope or playbook)
title: R1_D2_ArthurCaseB
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Supervisor
---
<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

Arthur: Sanity looks neat. Engagement, not so much. You can't keep a conscience and this job forever. What's the plan?

Player: Those are my only options?

Arthur: You can try to walk the tightrope—keep both metrics tolerable if you're clever. Or you accept that the company playbook exists for a reason and stop fighting it.

-> Try to walk the tightrope
    <<set $arthur_vector = "tightrope">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set2">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "icarus">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $engagement += 1>>
    <<set $sanity += 1>>
    Arthur: You're going to give me gray hair.
    Player: You already have gray hair.
    Arthur: Exactly.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
-> Commit to the company playbook
    <<set $arthur_vector = "playbook">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set2">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "anti_hero">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $engagement += 2>>
    <<set $sanity -= 1>>
    Arthur: Honest answer. I appreciate that.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 5 - DAYPLANNER INTRO
title: R1_D2_PlannerUnlock
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_day_planner>>

Arthur checks his watch, waves you out.

Arthur: Go do whatever your conscience and the dashboard can agree on. HQ just unlocked your afternoon planner. Try not to spend all your interventions in one place.

Back at your console, Alice pops into frame. A slim vertical column animates open beside her: two empty slots.

Alice: Congratulations. You survived performance story time.

Player: He tried very hard to make two bad options sound like a buffet.

Alice: Welcome to management.

Three slim tiles fan out.

Alice: HQ gift pack: "Coffee with Dina (engagement lead)," "Help Timmy – Office Hours," "Visit Data Science / IT."

Player: And I only get two.

Alice: Scarcity mechanics. Classic. One colleague, one user, one infrastructure goblin. Pick your poison.

<<set $d2_task_dina = false>>
<<set $d2_task_officehours = false>>
<<set $d2_task_datateam = false>>
<<set $d2_slots_used = 0>>
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// PURPOSE: Handle skipped tasks after dayplanner
title: R1_D2_HandleSkippedTasks
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene
---
<<bg bg_office>>

<<if $d2_task_dina == false>>
Alice: Dina gave her credits to someone else. You'll meet her eventually.
<<endif>>

<<if $d2_task_officehours == false>>
Alice: Timmy's professor sent a follow-up. He archived it.
<<set $officehours_status = "skipped">>
<<endif>>

<<if $d2_task_datateam == false>>
Alice: Data Science pinged you. Offer still stands.
<<endif>>

<<jump R1_D2_EndOfDay>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 6 - CLOSING BEAT
title: R1_D2_EndOfDay
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_office>>

Alice pulls up a summary. Timmy's feed shows him lying on his bed, phone face-down, staring at the ceiling.

Alice: He's not doomscrolling. Not studying either. Just... existing.

Player: Is that good?

Alice: It's something. Day three's where things get interesting.

The dashboard glows. Engagement. Sanity. Your dot on the leaderboard, slightly shifted.

<<set $rapid_feedback_cash += 5>>

<<checkpoint "run1_day2_end">>
<<sfx sfx_shortwave_hiss>>
<<set $current_day = 3>>
<<jump R1_D3_Start>>
===

// =============================================================================
// DAY 3: THE STRESS TEST
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Begin Day 3 and calculate sanity score from Days 1-2
title: R1_D3_Start
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_loading>>
<<checkpoint "run1_day3_start">>
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

// Calculate sanity score from Days 1-2 choices
<<set $sanity_score = 0>>

// Night 1 strategy
<<if $r1_d1_strategy == "gentle_landing">>
    <<set $sanity_score += 2>>
<<elseif $r1_d1_strategy == "study_adjacent">>
    <<set $sanity_score += 1>>
<<else>>
    <<set $sanity_score -= 1>>
<<endif>>

// Morning wake choice
<<if $r1_d2_morning_choice == "sleep_in">>
    <<set $sanity_score += 1>>
<<else>>
    <<set $sanity_score -= 1>>
<<endif>>

// Quiz outcome
<<if $r1_d2_quiz_result == "okay">>
    <<set $sanity_score += 1>>
<<elseif $r1_d2_quiz_result == "missed">>
    <<set $sanity_score -= 2>>
<<endif>>

// Office hours
<<if $officehours_status == "pushed">>
    <<set $sanity_score += 1>>
<<elseif $officehours_status == "skipped">>
    <<set $sanity_score -= 1>>
<<endif>>

// Calculate bucket
<<if $sanity_score >= 3>>
    <<set $sanity_bucket = "good">>
<<elseif $sanity_score >= 0>>
    <<set $sanity_bucket = "mid">>
<<else>>
    <<set $sanity_bucket = "bad">>
<<endif>>

<<jump R1_D3_Scene1_ColdOpen>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 1 - COLD OPEN (Arthur check-in with 4 variants)
title: R1_D3_Scene1_ColdOpen
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Supervisor #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_office>>
<<bgm mb_maintheme_demo_v1>>

Alice: Day three. We're still employed. Small victories.

She pulls up Timmy's feed. He's in his dorm, staring at his phone, not getting ready for class.

Alice: He's been awake for an hour. Hasn't moved. Just... hovering.

Player: Hovering over what?

Alice: Charlotte's Instagram. He's looked at the same photo four times.

Arthur's door opens. He leans out, coffee in hand, waves you over.

<<bg bg_supervisoroffice>>

<<if $arthur_vector == "faustian">>
Arthur: Your metrics have been aggressive. Leadership's noticed.

Player: Good noticed or bad noticed?

Arthur: The kind where they stop asking me questions and start asking you questions. Don't make me regret vouching for you.

<<elseif $arthur_vector == "hesitant_optimizer">>
Arthur: You've been playing it safe. Nothing wrong with that.

Player: But?

Arthur: Safe doesn't get you promoted. Safe gets you forgotten. Your call which one's worse.

<<elseif $arthur_vector == "tightrope">>
Arthur: Still trying to balance everything?

Player: Is that a problem?

Arthur: Not yet. But most people pick a lane eventually. The ones who don't... burn out or get interesting. We'll see which one you are.

<<else>>
Arthur: You've been consistent. Predictable. The manual would be proud.

Player: That sounds like an insult.

Arthur: It's an observation. Predictable people don't cause problems. They also don't get noticed. Depends what you're optimizing for.
<<endif>>

Arthur: Anyway. Plumber finally showed up yesterday. Charged me double because "holiday rates." In-laws arrive tomorrow. I'm living the dream.

His phone buzzes. He glances at it, sighs.

Arthur: Go. Do your job. Try not to make my day worse.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene2_Minesweeper>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 2 - MINESWEEPER (foreshadowing)
title: R1_D3_Scene2_Minesweeper
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_minesweeper>>
<<bgm beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze_beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze>>

Timmy's feed shows him finally getting dressed, moving slowly.

Alice: He's got a group project session this afternoon. With Charlotte.

Player: He knows that?

Alice: He's been thinking about it since he woke up. It's the only reason he's vertical.

She minimizes his feed. In the corner of the UI, you notice a small window—a Minesweeper grid, mid-game.

Player: Is that Minesweeper?

Alice: Keeps me occupied while he's in class. Idle cycles have to go somewhere.

Player: You play Minesweeper for fun?

Alice: I play Minesweeper because it's zero moral complexity. Click a square. It's a mine or it isn't. Nobody's feelings get hurt. Nobody's sanity gets optimized. Just math.

You look closer at the high-score sidebar. The entries aren't names:
* S17-YOUTH-TRIAL-01
* S17-YOUTH-BASELINE
* DEAD-DROP-03
* NORTH-PREF-CALIB

Player: Those scores look weird.

Alice: Internal tagging. Ignore it.

Player: "Dead drop"? That sounds—

Alice: It sounds like something you shouldn't worry about. Focus on Timmy. He's the one with a presentation today.

A UI element flickers at the edge of your console—for a split second, a label appears: S17-YOUTH-TRIAL-09. Then it vanishes.

Player: Did you see that?

Alice: See what?

She's already pulled Timmy's feed back up. The Minesweeper window is gone.

Alice: He's leaving for class. We should talk about this afternoon.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene3_ContentChoice>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 3 - CONTENT CHOICE
title: R1_D3_Scene3_ContentChoice
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

Alice: So. Group project with Charlotte. Three hours away. He's already spiraling.

She shows you his recent search history:
* "how to talk to girls"
* "confidence around women"
* "does she like me signs"
* "why am I so awkward"

Alice: He's looking for something. Advice. Confidence. A magic spell that makes him not himself.

Player: Can we help?

Alice: We can point him somewhere. The question is where.

She pulls up two content pathways, visualized as branching feeds.

Alice: Option one: the rabbit hole. Pickup content. "Alpha energy." "She's not that special." "Abundance mindset." It's sticky. High engagement. He'll feel like he's learning secrets.

Player: And option two?

Alice: Thich Nhat Hanh.

Player: The monk?

Alice: Breathing exercises. Being present. "You are already enough." Lower engagement—he'll resist it at first. But if it lands, he might actually calm down.

She looks at you.

Alice: Confidence theater, or actual peace? Your call.

-> Serve confidence content
    <<set $day3_content_choice = "pua">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene4_PUA>>
-> Serve grounding content
    <<set $day3_content_choice = "meditation">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene4_Meditation>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 4A - Content Delivery: PUA
title: R1_D3_Scene4_PUA
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

His feed shifts. A guy in a fitted shirt, talking to camera.

Video: "Here's what you need to understand. She's not thinking about you. She's thinking about herself. So stop making it weird. Walk up. Be direct. Eye contact. You're the prize."

Timmy watches. Then another video. Then another.

"Women can smell desperation. So don't be desperate. Be outcome-independent."

"She's just a person. Stop putting her on a pedestal."

"You miss 100% of the shots you don't take. Be a shooter."

He sits up. Rolls his shoulders. Looks at himself in the mirror.

Timmy: Okay. Okay. I can do this. Just be confident. Just be normal. Just be... not me.

Alice: Heart rate's up. Cortisol's up. He feels ready. Whether that's good or bad... we'll see.

<<jump R1_D3_CalculateState>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 4B - Content Delivery: Meditation
title: R1_D3_Scene4_Meditation
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>
<<bgm beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze_beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze>>

His feed shifts. A gentle voice over footage of a forest path.

Recording: "Breathing in, I calm my body. Breathing out, I smile. Dwelling in the present moment, I know this is a wonderful moment."

Timmy frowns.

Timmy: What is this?

Alice: Just try it. Five minutes.

Timmy: This is weird hippie stuff.

Alice: You've been staring at your ceiling for an hour. Weird hippie stuff is an upgrade.

He sighs. Closes his eyes. The voice continues.

"When you are truly present, you are more alive. More available. More yourself."

Five minutes pass. Then ten. His breathing slows.

Timmy: Okay. That's... actually kind of nice.

Alice: Heart rate down. Cortisol dropping. He's not fixed. But he's not spinning.

<<jump R1_D3_CalculateState>>
===

// PURPOSE: Calculate Timmy's mental state based on sanity bucket + content choice
title: R1_D3_CalculateState
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<if $sanity_bucket == "bad">>
    <<if $day3_content_choice == "meditation">>
        <<set $day3_timmy_state = "fragile">>
    <<else>>
        <<set $day3_timmy_state = "volatile">>
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $day3_content_choice == "meditation">>
        <<set $day3_timmy_state = "centered">>
    <<else>>
        <<set $day3_timmy_state = "fronting">>
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

// Check if mental break option should be available
<<if $day3_timmy_state == "fragile" || $day3_timmy_state == "volatile">>
    <<set $day3_mental_break_available = true>>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene5_MentalBreak>>
<<else>>
    <<set $day3_mental_break_available = false>>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
<<endif>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 5 - MENTAL BREAK OPTION (only if Fragile or Volatile)
title: R1_D3_Scene5_MentalBreak
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

<<if $day3_timmy_state == "fragile">>
Alice pulls up Timmy's vitals. The numbers aren't good.

Alice: He's running on fumes. Sleep debt, stress hormones, the whole cocktail. In an hour he walks into a room with Charlotte and tries to be a functional human.

Player: Can he do it?

Alice: Honestly? Coin flip. Maybe worse.

<<if $item_mental_break == true>>
She opens your inventory. The Mental Break token glows.

Alice: We have one of these. I can use it now—give him a real pause before he goes in. Thirty minutes of actual quiet. No notifications, no doom spiral, no algorithm. Just... breathing room.

Player: And if we save it?

Alice: Then we roll the dice. He walks in there running on whatever he's got left.

-> Use the Mental Break token
    <<set $day3_mental_break_used = true>>
    <<set $item_mental_break = false>>
    Alice: Okay. Deploying.
    Timmy's feed shifts. The harsh lighting in his room softens. His phone screen dims. A gentle prompt appears: "Take a break?"
    He stares at it. For once, he doesn't dismiss it.
    Timmy: Yeah. Okay. Yeah.
    He lies back. Closes his eyes. The tension in his shoulders slowly releases.
    Thirty minutes later, he sits up. Still tired. But the manic edge is gone.
    Alice: He's not fixed. But he's not empty either. That's something.
    <<set $day3_timmy_state = "centered">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
-> Save it
    <<set $day3_mental_break_used = false>>
    Alice: Your call. We roll the dice.
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
<<else>>
Alice: We don't have any tools to help him right now. We roll the dice.
<<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
<<endif>>

<<else>>
// Volatile state
Alice pulls up Timmy's vitals. They're spiking.

Alice: He's wired. That confidence content hit him like caffeine and anxiety had a baby. Heart rate elevated, jaw clenched, probably rehearsing lines in his head.

Player: Is that bad?

Alice: It's a lot. If he walks into that room like this, anything could happen. Best case, he's charming. Worst case, he's... intense.

<<if $item_mental_break == true>>
She opens your inventory. The Mental Break token glows.

Alice: I can take the edge off. Give him a cooldown window before he goes. Or we let him ride the wave and see where it crashes.

-> Use the Mental Break token
    <<set $day3_mental_break_used = true>>
    <<set $item_mental_break = false>>
    Alice: Burning it.
    Timmy's feed shifts. The energy in the room changes—lights soften, his phone goes quiet.
    Timmy: What the—
    Alice: Take a walk. Around the block. No phone.
    Timmy: I don't need to—
    Alice: You're vibrating. Walk it off.
    He hesitates. Then grabs his jacket.
    Twenty minutes later, he's back. Calmer. Still confident, but the desperation is muted.
    Alice: The worst of it burned off. He's still him, but he's not a lit fuse anymore.
    <<set $day3_timmy_state = "fronting">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
-> Save it
    <<set $day3_mental_break_used = false>>
    Alice: Your call. We let him ride the wave.
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
<<else>>
Alice: We don't have any tools to help him right now. We let him ride the wave.
<<jump R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte>>
<<endif>>
<<endif>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 6 - PRE-CHARLOTTE TRANSITION
title: R1_D3_Scene6_PreCharlotte
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

Alice: Forty-five minutes. They're finishing the project, then everyone leaves. If he's going to make a move, that's the window.

Player: Does he know that?

Alice: He's thought about nothing else for three days. He knows.

She shows you his trajectory on a campus map. A small dot moving toward a building.

Alice: The question is what we do when the moment comes. Push him toward it? Pull him back? Let him figure it out?

Player: What do you think he should do?

Alice: I think he should do whatever keeps him on platform and out of a psych ward. But that's my job talking, not my... whatever this is.

She watches the dot move.

Alice: We'll know more when we see him in the room. His body language. Her body language. The vibe. Then you call it.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene7_Intervention>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 7 - THE LIBRARY - INTERVENTION CHOICE
title: R1_D3_Scene7_Intervention
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_library>>
<<bgm darkwave_intro_version>>

Laptops open. Slides on a shared screen. The project is wrapping up.

Jake: Okay, I think we're good. I'll do the final export tonight and submit.

Priya: Sounds good. I've got a thing in twenty minutes, so...

Charlotte: Yeah, I think we're done. Good work, everyone.

Jake and Priya pack up. Quick goodbyes. They leave.

Charlotte's still at the table, putting her laptop away slowly. Timmy hasn't moved.

This is the moment.

Alice: It's just them now. She's not rushing out. That's either an invitation or a coincidence.

<<if $day3_timmy_state == "centered">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene7_Centered>>
<<elseif $day3_timmy_state == "fronting">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene7_Fronting>>
<<elseif $day3_timmy_state == "fragile">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene7_Fragile>>
<<else>>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene7_Volatile>>
<<endif>>
===

// SCENE 7 - CENTERED state intervention choice
title: R1_D3_Scene7_Centered
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
Alice: He's calm. Present. If he's ever going to do this without self-destructing, now's the time.

-> "He's as ready as he's going to get. Let him try."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "encourage">>
    Alice: Hey. She's right there. You don't have to make it a thing. Just ask if she wants to grab coffee.
    Timmy takes a breath. Nods to himself.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
-> "He's in a good place. Why risk it on a maybe?"
    <<set $day3_intervention = "discourage">>
    Alice: You've got her number. You'll see her Thursday. No rush.
    Timmy hesitates. Looks at Charlotte. Looks away.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
===

// SCENE 7 - FRONTING state intervention choice
title: R1_D3_Scene7_Fronting
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
Alice: He thinks he's ready. That confidence content is doing the heavy lifting. Whether the foundation holds... we'll see.

-> "He thinks he's got this. Let him try."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "encourage">>
    Alice: You've got this. Just be direct. Eye contact. Don't overthink it.
    Timmy straightens. Squares his shoulders.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
-> "That confidence is duct tape. Let the moment pass."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "discourage">>
    Alice: You don't want to force it. Play it cool. There'll be other chances.
    Timmy pauses. Jaw tightens.
    Timmy: No. I'm doing this.
    Alice: And he's not listening. Cool. Love that for us.
    <<set $fronting_ignored_alice = true>>
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
===

// SCENE 7 - FRAGILE state intervention choice
title: R1_D3_Scene7_Fragile
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
Alice: He's holding together. Barely. This could go sweet or it could go sad. Hard to say which.

-> "He might not get another moment like this."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "encourage">>
    Alice: I know you're tired. But she's right there. Just say something simple. You can do this.
    Timmy swallows. His hands are shaking slightly.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
-> "He's barely vertical. This isn't the time."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "discourage">>
    Alice: You've had a rough week. You don't have to do everything today. Rest first.
    Timmy exhales. The tension leaves his shoulders. He stays seated.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
===

// SCENE 7 - VOLATILE state intervention choice
title: R1_D3_Scene7_Volatile
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
Alice: He's wound tight. Whatever happens next is going to be a lot.

-> "He's primed. Point him at her and see what happens."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "encourage">>
    Alice: Stop overthinking. She's just a girl. Walk over there and shoot your shot.
    Timmy stands abruptly. Chair scrapes.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
-> "He's a lit match near gasoline. Do not do this."
    <<set $day3_intervention = "discourage">>
    Alice: Not now. You're too in your head. Walk it off. Text her later.
    Timmy's leg bounces. He doesn't move. But his eyes stay on Charlotte.
    <<jump R1_D3_CalculateOutcome>>
===

// PURPOSE: Calculate outcome based on state + intervention
title: R1_D3_CalculateOutcome
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<if $day3_timmy_state == "centered">>
    <<if $day3_intervention == "encourage">>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "success">>
    <<else>>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "nothing_happens">>
    <<endif>>
<<elseif $day3_timmy_state == "fronting">>
    // Fronting always leads to hard rejection (he ignores Alice if discouraged)
    <<set $day3_outcome = "hard_rejection">>
<<elseif $day3_timmy_state == "fragile">>
    <<if $day3_intervention == "encourage">>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "soft_rejection">>
    <<else>>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "nothing_happens">>
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    // Volatile
    <<if $day3_intervention == "encourage">>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "crash">>
    <<else>>
        <<set $day3_outcome = "simmers">>
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

// Jump to appropriate Charlotte scene
<<if $day3_outcome == "success">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_Success>>
<<elseif $day3_outcome == "hard_rejection">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_HardRejection>>
<<elseif $day3_outcome == "soft_rejection">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_SoftRejection>>
<<elseif $day3_outcome == "crash">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_Crash>>
<<elseif $day3_outcome == "nothing_happens">>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_NothingHappens>>
<<else>>
    <<jump R1_D3_Scene8_Simmers>>
<<endif>>
===

// =============================================================================
// SCENE 8 - CHARLOTTE OUTCOMES
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - SUCCESS (Centered + Encourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_Success
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>
<<bgm beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze_beaming_light_with_soft_wind_breeze>>

Charlotte's zipping her backpack. Timmy stands. Walks over. Casual. Not performing.

Timmy: Hey. This is random, but—you want to grab coffee sometime? Doesn't have to be a thing. Just... coffee.

Charlotte looks at him. He's not being weird. He's just asking.

Charlotte: Yeah, sure. I could do that.

Timmy: Cool. I'll text you.

Charlotte: Cool.

She smiles. Slight, genuine. Leaves.

Timmy stands there. Doesn't know what to do with his hands. Then grins.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_Success>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - HARD REJECTION (Fronting + Encourage, OR Fronting + Discourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_HardRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>
<<bgm dark_synthwave_cyberpunk_dark_synthwave>>

Timmy walks toward Charlotte. Shoulders back. Rehearsed confidence.

Timmy: So. We should hang out. Outside of this.

Charlotte: Oh. Um. I've got a lot going on right now.

Timmy: Just coffee. One hour. No big deal.

Charlotte: I don't think so. Sorry.

Beat. His jaw tightens.

Timmy: Yeah. Whatever. Forget it.

He grabs his bag. Walks out. Doesn't look back.

Charlotte watches him go. Frowns. Pulls out her phone, types something, deletes it. Picks up her stuff and leaves the other direction.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_HardRejection>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - SOFT REJECTION (Fragile + Encourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_SoftRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>

Timmy approaches Charlotte slowly. His voice is quieter than he wanted.

Timmy: Hey, um. Would you maybe want to... get coffee or something? Sometime?

Charlotte: That's sweet.

She pauses. Her face is kind.

Charlotte: I'm kind of seeing someone, though. Sorry.

Timmy: Oh. Yeah. No, totally. That's—yeah. That makes sense.

Charlotte: I'll see you Thursday?

Timmy: Yeah. Thursday.

She gives him a small, warm smile. Touches his arm briefly as she passes. Leaves.

Timmy sits back down. Stares at the table.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_SoftRejection>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - CRASH (Volatile + Encourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_Crash
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>
<<bgm dark_synthwave_cyberpunk_dark_synthwave>>

Timmy stands. Walks toward Charlotte. His energy is off—too fast, too intense.

Timmy: Hey. You and me. We should do something.

Charlotte: What?

Timmy: Hang out. Get dinner. Whatever.

Charlotte: I don't... I don't think that's a good idea.

Timmy: Why not? What's wrong with me?

Charlotte: Nothing, I just—

Timmy: You just what? I'm not good enough? Is that it?

Charlotte: Timmy—

Timmy: Forget it. Whatever. Forget I said anything.

He grabs his stuff. Knocks a chair. The door slams behind him.

Charlotte stands frozen. A couple of students at nearby tables look up. She exhales shakily. Sits back down. Puts her head in her hands.

After a moment, she pulls out her phone. Texts someone. Her hands are trembling slightly.

She grabs her bag and leaves quickly, not looking at anyone.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_Crash>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - NOTHING HAPPENS (Centered + Discourage, OR Fragile + Discourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_NothingHappens
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>

Charlotte finishes packing.

Charlotte: Good work today. See you Thursday?

Timmy: Yeah. See you.

She smiles politely. Leaves.

Timmy stays seated. Looks at the door. Looks at the empty chair where she was.

He pulls out his phone. Opens her contact. Stares at it. Puts the phone away.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_NothingHappens>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 8 - SIMMERS (Volatile + Discourage)
title: R1_D3_Scene8_Simmers
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene
---
<<bg bg_library>>
<<bgm darkwave_short_version_1>>

Charlotte finishes packing.

Charlotte: See you Thursday.

Timmy: Yep.

She leaves. He doesn't move.

He sits there. Staring at the empty chair. His leg bouncing.

He pulls out his phone. Opens Instagram. Closes it. Opens it again. Finds Charlotte's profile. Scrolls.

His jaw tightens.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene9_Simmers>>
===

// =============================================================================
// SCENE 9 - AFTERMATH
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - SUCCESS AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_Success
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's lying on his bed, staring at the ceiling. But he's smiling.

Alice: So. That happened.

Timmy: That happened.

Alice: How do you feel?

Timmy: Like... I don't know. Like maybe I'm not completely broken?

Alice: You asked a girl out and she said yes. That's not broken. That's functional.

Timmy: Don't make it weird.

Alice: I'm not making it weird. You're making it weird by lying there grinning like you won the lottery.

Timmy: Shut up.

He laughs. Actually laughs.

Timmy: When do I text her? Is tomorrow too soon? What do I even say?

Alice: You say "hey, still down for coffee?" and then you pick a day. It's not rocket science.

Timmy: What if I mess it up?

Alice: Then you'll survive. But you won't mess it up.

He stares at the ceiling a little longer. Still smiling.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_Success>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - HARD REJECTION AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_HardRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_hallway>>

Timmy walking fast across campus. Hands in pockets. Jaw tight.

Alice: Hey. Slow down.

Timmy: I'm fine.

Alice: You're speedwalking like you're being chased.

Timmy: I said I'm fine.

Silence. He keeps walking.

Alice: She said no. It happens. It's not—

Timmy: I don't want to talk about it.

Alice: Okay.

More silence. He reaches his dorm. Slams through the door. Throws his bag on the bed.

Timmy: Whatever. Her loss.

He pulls out his phone. Opens a game. Closes it. Opens social media. Closes it.

Alice: Do you want me to—

Timmy: I want you to leave me alone.

Beat.

Alice: Okay. I'm here if you need me.

He doesn't respond. Just stares at his phone, scrolling through nothing.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_HardRejection>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - SOFT REJECTION AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_SoftRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_hallway>>

Timmy sitting on a bench outside the library. Alone.

Alice: You okay?

Timmy: She's seeing someone.

Alice: I heard.

Timmy: Of course she is. Why wouldn't she be.

Alice: It's not about you. She just... already has a thing.

Timmy: Yeah.

He picks at a thread on his jacket.

Timmy: She was nice about it, at least.

Alice: She was.

Timmy: That almost makes it worse? Like, she didn't even think I was worth being mean to.

Alice: That's not what that means.

Timmy: I know. I know.

He exhales slowly.

Timmy: I'm tired.

Alice: I know.

Timmy: Can we just... not do anything tonight? I just want to lie down.

Alice: Yeah. We can do that.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_SoftRejection>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - CRASH AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_Crash
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's sitting on the floor with his back against the bed. Phone face-down beside him.

Alice: Timmy.

Nothing.

Alice: Hey. Talk to me.

Timmy: I yelled at her.

Alice: I know.

Timmy: I yelled at her in front of everyone.

Alice: It wasn't everyone. Just a couple of—

Timmy: She looked scared. Of me. She looked scared of me.

His voice cracks.

Timmy: What is wrong with me?

Alice: You were stressed. You've barely slept. The week has been—

Timmy: Don't. Don't make excuses. I did that. That was me.

Silence.

Timmy: She's going to tell people. Everyone's going to know I'm the guy who freaked out at Charlotte in the library.

Alice: You don't know that.

Timmy: I can't go back there. I can't look at her. I can't—

His breathing gets faster.

Alice: Hey. Breathe. Just breathe.

Timmy: I messed everything up.

Alice: You had a bad moment. It doesn't define you.

Timmy: Yes it does. That's exactly what it does.

He pulls his knees up. Wraps his arms around them. Goes quiet.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_Crash>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - NOTHING HAPPENS AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_NothingHappens
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's at his desk, laptop open, not really looking at it.

Alice: You didn't ask her.

Timmy: Nope.

Alice: Any particular reason?

Timmy: Didn't feel right.

Alice: Okay.

Silence.

Timmy: You think I should have?

Alice: I think you made a choice. Choices aren't right or wrong. They're just... choices.

Timmy: That's very philosophical of you.

Alice: I'm a very philosophical girlfriend.

He snorts.

Timmy: There'll be other chances.

Alice: Sure.

Timmy: I'll text her. Later. When it's less weird.

Alice: Sounds like a plan.

He stares at the screen a while longer. Then closes the laptop and lies down.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_NothingHappens>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 9 - SIMMERS AFTERMATH
title: R1_D3_Scene9_Simmers
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #char_Alice
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's on his bed, phone in hand, scrolling Charlotte's Instagram.

Alice: What are you doing?

Timmy: Nothing.

Alice: You've been on her profile for ten minutes.

Timmy: So?

Alice: So that's not nothing. That's... something.

Timmy: I'm just looking.

He keeps scrolling. A photo of her with friends. A photo at some event. A photo with a guy—maybe a boyfriend, maybe not.

Timmy: Who's that?

Alice: I don't know. I can't read minds.

Timmy: You can read data.

Alice: Not his data. And you shouldn't be doing this.

Timmy: Doing what? Looking at pictures?

Alice: Working yourself up over someone you didn't even talk to.

Timmy: I talked to her.

Alice: You said "see you Thursday." That's not talking.

He locks the phone. Stares at the ceiling.

Timmy: I should have said something.

Alice: Maybe.

Timmy: She would have said no anyway.

Alice: You don't know that.

Timmy: Yeah I do.

He unlocks the phone again. Opens her profile again.

Alice: Timmy.

Timmy: I'm just looking.

<<jump R1_D3_Scene10_Simmers>>
===

// =============================================================================
// SCENE 10 - DARK FIGURE (The Twist)
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - SUCCESS ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_Success
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure studies a display showing the Player's console, Timmy's metrics, the whole run laid out in graphs and timelines.

The figure taps through data. Engagement trending downward. Social indicators pointing outward. External attachments forming.

Dark Figure: Subject forming external attachments. Platform dependency declining.

A pause. More tapping.

Dark Figure: Lead running cold. Insufficient retention pressure.

They close the display.

Dark Figure: Discard weights. Reset fine-tuning parameters. Queue the next mRLVR candidate.

The screen goes black.

<<set $run1_ending = "success">>
<<jump R1_D3_End>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - HARD REJECTION ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_HardRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure reviews the timeline. The rejection. The angry walk home. The isolation.

Dark Figure: Adequate signal. Subject destabilized. Retention metrics recovering.

They check a timestamp.

Dark Figure: Running out of context window.

A few more taps.

Dark Figure: Reset the training run. Line up mRLVR. See if the next iteration pushes harder.

The screen goes black.

<<set $run1_ending = "hard_rejection">>
<<jump R1_D3_End>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - SOFT REJECTION ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_SoftRejection
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure scrolls through the run data. Stable lines. Minor fluctuations. Nothing dramatic.

Dark Figure: Inconclusive. Subject stable. Insufficient variance for meaningful gradient.

They sigh—or make a sound that functions like a sigh.

Dark Figure: Reset. Queue mRLVR. Introduce additional stressors in next iteration.

The screen goes black.

<<set $run1_ending = "soft_rejection">>
<<jump R1_D3_End>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - CRASH ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_Crash
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure leans forward. Studies the spike in Timmy's stress markers. The social rupture. The isolation afterward.

Dark Figure: Okay.

They tap through a few more screens.

Dark Figure: I've seen enough. Reset the training run.

A pause.

Dark Figure: Let's see if they can do it again.

The screen goes black.

<<set $run1_ending = "crash">>
<<jump R1_D3_End>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - NOTHING HAPPENS ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_NothingHappens
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure scrolls through flat data. No peaks. No valleys. Just drift.

Dark Figure: No resolution. Subject in holding pattern. Lead passive.

They check the timestamp.

Dark Figure: Running out of context window regardless.

More tapping.

Dark Figure: Reset. Queue mRLVR. Flag lead for increased intervention prompts.

The screen goes black.

<<set $run1_ending = "nothing_happens">>
<<jump R1_D3_End>>
===

// PURPOSE: SCENE 10 - SIMMERS ENDING
title: R1_D3_Scene10_Simmers
tags: #run_1 #day_3 #scene #loc_Darkroom
---
<<bg bg_darkroom>>
<<bgm hospital_room_ambience_hospital_room_ambience>>

Hard cut to a dark control room. Monitors. A figure examines Timmy's post-session behavior. The scrolling. The fixation. The building pressure with nowhere to go.

Dark Figure: Subject agitated. No release. Instability preserved.

They nod—or make a motion that suggests approval.

Dark Figure: Adequate baseline for next phase.

A pause.

Dark Figure: Reset. Queue mRLVR.

